  "text": "We are given a **geoboard** with coordinates ranging from **(0,0)** to **(6,6)**, with each unit distance between adjacent points.\n\nWe are to find the number of **triangles** $ABC$ such that:\n\n- Triangle $ABC$ is a **right triangle**.\n- The **side lengths** of the triangle are **2 units**.\n- The **hypotenuse** is **not parallel to any side of the geoboard**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the side length constraint\n\nSince the side length of the triangle is 2 units, and the triangle is a **right triangle**, we must choose two points at a distance of **2** from each other, and the third point must lie such that the triangle is a right triangle.\n\nSo, we need to find all **right triangles** with legs of length 2 units, such that:\n\n- The hypotenuse is **not parallel to any side of the geoboard**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Geometric constraints\n\nOn a **geoboard**, each unit square has sides of length 1, and the total grid is 6x6 points. The side length of the triangle is **2 units**, so we need to choose points that are **2 units apart** and form a right triangle.\n\nThis can happen in **two possible configurations** for a right triangle with legs of length 2:\n\n- One leg is horizontal, one is vertical.\n- The hypotenuse is the diagonal of a 2x2 square (since the leg lengths are 2 units).\n\nSo, we need to count the number of right triangles with legs of length 2, **not aligned with the sides of the geoboard**, and with hypotenuse **not parallel to any side**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Count valid triangles\n\nWe can approach this systematically:\n\n1. Find all right triangles with legs of length 2, and hypotenuse not aligned with any side.\n2. Subtract those that are aligned with the sides of the geoboard.\n\nLet’s find how many right triangles with legs 2 are there on the 6x6 grid.\n\n- There are **6** horizontal and **6** vertical lines.\n- Each horizontal line has **5** points, so there are **6 × 5 = 30** horizontal lines.\n- Similarly, there are **6 × 5 = 30** vertical lines.\n- Each right triangle has one horizontal and one vertical leg, so there are **6 × 6 = 36** possible right triangles.\n\nNow, subtract those that are aligned with the geoboard’s sides.\n\n- The right triangles that are aligned with the sides have legs of length 2 and are aligned with the grid.\n- There are **30** such triangles (one per horizontal and one per vertical line).\n\nSo, total valid triangles = **36 - 30 = 6**.\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{6}\n$$",
